        // The mutant allows no deposit, so balanceBeforeSettle could be just the initial 1 ether.

        // After settle (if player wins), contract sends 2 ether. 
        // The invariant: initial balance (1 ether) + sum(deposits) - sum(payouts) >= 0 at all times.
        // But we can't see deposits individually. 

        // Better rule: The player's net ether change after the whole sequence (lockInGuess + settle) cannot be positive.
        // In the reference, if the player deposited 1 ether and won, they get 2 ether back => net +1 ether.
        // In the mutant, if they deposit 0 and win, they get 2 ether => net +2 ether.

        // Compute player's ether balance change
        uint256 playerBalanceAfter = player.balance;
        // Player started with 3 ether, sent 1 ether to lockInGuess
        // So net change = (playerBalanceAfter - 3 ether) + (if settle sent ether, that's included in playerBalanceAfter)

        // General rule: the player's net ether gain cannot exceed 1 ether (the max possible when they deposited 1 ether)
        // For the reference: net gain <= 1 ether
        // For the mutant: net gain can be up to 2 ether

        // Since we sent exactly 1 ether in lockInGuess (as per reference), the mutant also allows 1 ether
        // But the mutation is that the require was deleted, so anyone can call lockInGuess with any value.
        // In our test, we sent 1 ether, so both reference and mutant behave the same.

